Establish the code-backed contract of the existing canonical-data/frontend-projection implementation. Trace runtime::UnlockedData<T>, its write notifier and moves, frontend::Data/FrontEnd registration and lookup, TuiFrontEnd initialization/data_changed, and ModelSelection and SessionStats including out-of-line definitions. Locate definitions with findsymbol and follow callers. Explain owners, permitted threads, lock/notification ordering, stable addresses, detached states, session replacement and destruction; distinguish verified properties from unenforced intentions.

Explain dirty_.exchange(false), concurrent publication, independent accumulated change flags, initial/unknown values, full copying of these bounded domains, cached string-reference lifetime and numeric RefreshResult values. Investigate dependent projections, particularly SessionStats consuming ModelSelection context metadata, single-consumer assumptions, lost notification risks and cross-domain coherence. Atomic dirtiness alone guarantees neither object lifetime nor transactional reads. This mechanism is for backend-affecting state that is or may become visible, not UI-only appearance/interaction or drafts: those belong in tui-specific files on the tui-thread without locks/UnlockedData, with draft text crossing only as a submitted string. Session history is too large for this copying model; preserve its existing history/streaming transport as an explicit deferred exception, leaving future backend list/vector access to separate design.

Produce a reference-contract artifact registered in contents, with file/symbol references, regression coverage, invariants, limitations and verification questions. Identify backend-header exposure and the smallest needed boundary changes. Investigation only: do not introduce a competing registry, generic state bag, new history journal or notification architecture. Unresolved assumptions must remain explicit for reconciliation.
